e registered direction; however, their point estimates are below the clinical threshold. H17 is the most promising of this group because outcome_008 with feature_002 has a signed estimate of +0.0149, about 53% of the clinical threshold, and an upper bound above threshold, but the interval still includes zero and substantial null and below-threshold values. H15, H16, and H19 are similar but less informative and are deferred. H26 is very imprecise relative to the small outcome_014 clinical threshold, with an interval extending to +0.032 while the threshold is only +0.0093; it is deferred rather than followed up.\n\nA critical review supports restraint in validation. These are exploratory unadjusted comparisons after many tests, with opaque predictor labels, no confounder adjustment, and no causal interpretation. Requesting independent validation for H17 would be inefficient because the point estimate is below the clinical threshold and the independent interval would likely be wider; the previous independent validation of H5 already demonstrated that discovery signals near the threshold may fail. The remaining validation budget is better reserved for a candidate with point estimate above threshold or for a later replication of a strong signal. I therefore request no validation at this stage.\n\nTo improve coverage rather than over-interrogate weak signals, I registered feature_034 marginal tests on the three outcomes not yet screened, outcome_012, outcome_015, and outcome_016. The next analysis block should test those new proposals together with the untested existing feature_002/feature_060 or feature_034 marginal comparisons for outcome_014, outcome_004, outcome_002, outcome_003, outcome_005, outcome_007, and outcome_009. If this broader screen still yields no threshold-crossing, statistically supported candidate, the next useful pivot is to test additional balanced binary or categorical predictors, such as feature_054, across the most favorable core outcomes. All current conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal effects or generalizable findings.",
